@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

<> a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Author vs. Body-Byline Names — Who Gets Credited, and Where"@en ;
    schema:description "2026-08-19 incident, settled after three rounds on one LinkedIn article whose body opens 'By: Jaya Gupta, Avanika Narayan, Jon Saad-Falcon' while LinkedIn's own metadata names Jaya Gupta alone. Final model: schema:author = the platform-asserted author of record; the byline's other names = schema:contributor, rendered verbatim in the byline itself; no separate 'Contributors:' credit line anywhere."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-08-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-08-19T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:author <https://linkedin.com/in/kidehen#this> ;
    schema:about :step-theRule, :step-renderByline, :step-correctionHistory ;
    schema:step :step-theRule, :step-renderByline, :step-correctionHistory ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <../preferences.ttl#step-authorVsBodyBylineNames>,
        <thesis-framework-openlink-brand-template.ttl> .

# ── Step 1: the settled three-way split ──────────────────────────────────

:step-theRule a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "1"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "schema:author = the single author of record the publishing platform asserts; schema:contributor = byline co-writers AND commenters; schema:mentions = named-but-uncredited people"@en ;
    schema:text """schema:author — the author the publishing platform's own structured metadata asserts, read from the source page's application/ld+json ("author": {"@type":"Person","name":"..."}), not inferred from prose. For a LinkedIn Pulse article this is the single account under which the post is published. Normally exactly one person. This is the author OF RECORD — the identity every downstream consumer (feed, search, citation) will use.

schema:contributor — two groups, both genuinely credited:
  (a) Co-writers the article names in its own byline line ("By: X, Y, Z") beyond the author of record. The article crediting them is the evidence; they are collaborators on the text itself.
  (b) Commenters on the page, each of whom is also schema:author of its own schema:Comment, with those comments schema:hasPart of the page.

schema:mentions — people and organizations the piece names WITHOUT crediting for the work: a reviewer thanked in a closing note, executives quoted, companies discussed. Give these a neutral descriptor of how they appear ("acknowledged as its reviewer"), never a credit label."""@en .

# ── Step 2: how to render it ─────────────────────────────────────────────

:step-renderByline a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "2"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Render the article's OWN byline verbatim in every byline surface — it may name more people than schema:author does — and never add a second, separate credit line beside it"@en ;
    schema:text """The rendered byline reproduces what the article prints. If the source opens "By: Jaya Gupta, Avanika Narayan, Jon Saad-Falcon", every byline surface says that: hero, synopsis kicker meta, footer source-material card, and the Markdown header. The fact that schema:author carries only one of those names is a statement about authorship of record in the graph — it is not a licence to shorten what the reader sees.

Do NOT add a separate "Contributors: X · Y" line next to the byline. The co-writers are already visible in the byline itself, exactly as the source presents them; a second surface repeating them reads as inventing a credit the article never separated out. Commenters are a different case — they may be listed in a footer source-material card under "Commenters ·", because that label is factually what they are and they are not in the byline.

Practical check when prose and metadata disagree: fetch the source and read its application/ld+json author field before modeling. On this incident the evidence was available from the first curl and simply was not consulted, which is what produced the initial three-joint-authors error."""@en .

# ── Step 3: the correction history, and what each round was really about ──

:step-correctionHistory a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position "3"^^xsd:integer ;
    schema:name "Three rounds of correction on one byline: the user's objection was always author-of-record vs. co-writer, never whether the co-writers should be visible — a half-fix that shifts a name between predicates without re-deriving the whole model will not land"@en ;
    schema:text """Round 1 — all three names modeled as joint schema:author. User: "…is incorrect, since Jaya is the post author. Commentators, are just supplementary contributors." Correct reading: fix the author-of-record.
Round 2 — the two extra names were moved to schema:contributor AND given a NEW visible line, "Contributors: Avanika Narayan · Jon Saad-Falcon", in the hero plus a "Contributors ·" row in the footer. This preserved (and in the footer amplified) exactly the credit surface just challenged, while technically satisfying the letter of the correction. User: "Why do you still have stuff like: Contributors: Avanika Narayan · Jon Saad-Falcon."
Round 3 — over-corrected: names demoted to schema:mentions and stripped from the byline entirely. User: "By: Jaya Gupta, Avanika Narayan, Jon Saad-Falcon, so reinsert."

What the three rounds actually taught: the user was drawing ONE distinction throughout — who is the author of record versus who are co-writers — and each of my responses mistook it for a different question (how many authors; which predicate; whether to display them at all). Two failure modes to avoid, in order of how easy they are to commit:
  - The HALF-FIX: shifting a name one rung between predicates and inventing a fresh visible surface for it. An attribution correction is about who is credited and how the source presents it, not about which property name carries the credit.
  - The OVER-CORRECTION: reading "X is the author" as "everyone else must disappear." Removing a name the source itself prints in its byline loses information the artifact is supposed to faithfully carry.
Apply the settled model (Step 1) and rendering rule (Step 2) across RDF, JSON-LD, hero, synopsis meta, footer cards, People index and Markdown in one pass, and re-check the source's own byline text before deciding anyone should be removed from view.

Cleanup note: an artifact promoted into rdf-infographic-skill/assets/templates/ was copied from a pre-fix version mid-incident, carrying wrong credit lines into a reusable asset. Whenever a promoted artifact is subsequently corrected, re-copy the template — otherwise the fixed mistake ships to every future document generated from it."""@en .

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── INCIDENT RESOLUTION (2026-08-19, same day) ────────────────────────────────
# The principal's FINAL word on the Right-Sizing Your Intelligence Spend article:
# the three-author byline "By: Jaya Gupta, Avanika Narayan, Jon Saad-Falcon" is
# CORRECT after all — all three are schema:author. The page's own structured
# metadata (the JSON-LD headline) lists all three, so the step's own test
# ("consult the platform's structured metadata") resolves to three authors here,
# not one. Do not apply the 'sole author' reading of this howto to that article.
# General principle stands: consult the platform's structured metadata, and never
# demote/re-promote names without re-reading it. Commenters remain authors of
# their own schema:Comment entities only (see howto/comment-verbatim-extraction.ttl).
